<header><p class="enc-kicker">Term · language</p><h1 class="enc-title">Semitic languages</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Semitic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Semitic languages are a family of languages of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> that includes Akkadian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, Phoenician, Arabic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geez">Ge'ez</a>. Akkadian was the first Semitic language to be widely written, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> profile notes its role in administration alongside Sumerian. Phoenician, a Semitic language, was the language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a>.</p></div>
